            {
              "type": "reasoning.summary",
              "summary": "**Calculating peer forecasts**\n\nI'm reviewing our previous forecast and how it compares to peers' values. The task involves collective forecasting, likely needing to average five peer reports. In prediction markets, the average forecasts of equal analysts are used. \n\nI calculated a mean implied p value. I think combining this with my own value seems straightforward, but generative information makes it more complex. Peer confidence values might relate to their forecasts, and I should consider how to count each observation accurately.**Interpreting survey signals**\n\nIt seems like all raw survey signals are pooled, so peer answers shouldn't be counted as separate observations. Since only my own surveys are shown, those peer reports don't expose individual observations. However, I can still use the peer reports as evidence. \n\nThe instruction indicates that I shouldn't reconstruct unknown details. My posterior should be based on the shown generative model. While peer reports may not specify a decision rule, their stated probabilities could serve as useful ancillary information. Each report might be viewed as a noisy estimate of demand, considering overall conditional independence.**Modeling recommendations**\n\nI’m thinking about how to model each recommendation as a single observation with its reliability. The guideline warns that each observation should only be counted once, which means I might not use both answer and confidence as separate signals. Instead, confidence can serve as reliability. \n\nThis leads me to combine reports as forecast signals rather than raw surveys. I could calculate my values alongside peer forecasts using Bayesian evidence, treating their recommendation accuracy as reliability based on their private observations. Each report's likelihood could be defined using their confidence levels.",
